{"event_type":"review","id":"50bc10eee270027fd4206fc7016763e6b5f342b421e90fdc494ecea856e036b6","occurred_at":"2026-11-07T16:17:22Z","payload":{"artifacts":[{"location":"/home/ctaylor/.local/share/crq-backfill/artifacts/sha256/176a73078ae018869752172b2fece2ce01d65e5015674f655bf8a8f68fbd3af0","sha256":"176a73078ae018869752172b2fece2ce01d65e5015674f655bf8a8f68fbd3af0"},{"location":"/home/ctaylor/.local/share/crq-backfill/artifacts/sha256/d8ed648cacb55a749970c935986310acddfafb2817d6ab0944d03f9bdae6e7d5","sha256":"d8ed648cacb55a749970c935986310acddfafb2817d6ab0944d03f9bdae6e7d5"},{"kind":"git-bundle","location":"/home/ctaylor/.local/share/crq-backfill/artifacts/sha256/ed0d486353ec349a99d1350158f55262c9461308a150379e6f4231580e965361","sha256":"ed0d486353ec349a99d1350158f55262c9461308a150379e6f4231580e965361"},{"location":"/home/ctaylor/.local/share/crq-backfill/artifacts/sha256/449161afd05b4c5567606d5484323ed14dae8be6647fcb09926736b8977f9f88","sha256":"449161afd05b4c5567606d5484323ed14dae8be6647fcb09926736b8977f9f88"},{"location":"/home/ctaylor/.local/share/crq-backfill/artifacts/sha256/af45d095ed502940d6f2841aab916483dc53f2996d6599dbe71c1bd447198b6f","sha256":"af45d095ed502940d6f2841aab916483dc53f2996d6599dbe71c1bd447198b6f"}],"attempt_id":"00f2d14e1cc5ca4bcd3d091df89e8fa7ee30b061e317fd45e6bf674532637d08:0:1","base":"e4cc615e76a995131f7f0ad8bc2bac1791fc8be9","completion":{"errors":[],"exit_code":0,"scope_verified":true,"skipped":false,"terminal":true},"findings":[{"anchor_status":"unverified","blob":"05ed00b54a7a50c3e6aced453ad7465a1993f62d","body":"**`count('failures')` also matches the `expected failures` token, so an `OK` Python run can be reported as `fail`.**\n\nThe pattern `\\bfailures=(\\d+)` matches the `failures=N` inside `expected failures=N`, because `\\b` sits between the space and `f`. unittest prints `failures=` first when real failures exist, so that case parses correctly. When there are no real failures, the first match is the `expected failures` count.\n\nExample: stderr ending in `OK (expected failures=1)`.\n- Line 216 counts `failed = 1`, and line 217 counts `skipped = 1`.\n- `passed` becomes `total - 2`, which can be negative.\n- `pythonRunVerdict` (line 229) then returns `result: 'fail'` because `counts.failed > 0`.\n\nAny module that uses `@unittest.expectedFailure` fails the gate on a clean run.\n\nAnchor each key to a token boundary inside the parenthesized list. The list items are separated by `, `.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-  const count = (key) => Number(new RegExp(`\\\\b${key}=(\\\\d+)`).exec(last[3] ?? '')?.[1] ?? 0);\n+  const count = (key) =>\n+    Number(new RegExp(`(?:^|, )${key}=(\\\\d+)`).exec(last[3] ?? '')?.[1] ?? 0);\n```\n</details>\n\nAdd a regression case for `OK (expected failures=1)` and `OK (skipped=1, expected failures=1)` beside the existing `readPythonReport` tests.","id":"627e60dac49d1428043476eb3fd1e66ba82922fdd410467c22d6209efe0f7632","intervals":[],"path":"scripts/lib/test-narrow.mjs","severity":"minor","source_id":null,"status":"untriaged","title":"`count('failures')` also matches the `expected failures` token, so an `OK` Python run can be reported as `fail`."}],"head":"2542fb281e9dfd3a969b02fb2035a24028cf4ab2","head_tree":"caf2bd1c0f6a76b490442f34307539ee3268e814","input_scope":[{"blob":"83d3c940e2cf46cdcf39dca4c8a705f802c988a0","intervals":[[1,1]],"path":"scripts/lib/dist-tests.d.mts"},{"blob":"2773185c6a3f7da2040647a8d36fbe4b68e0cef4","intervals":[[1,23]],"path":"scripts/lib/dist-tests.mjs"},{"blob":"3a2b2b77f01c8b0bc09bb9fc84adc0706dafa824","intervals":[[1,93]],"path":"scripts/lib/resource-scope.mjs"},{"blob":"e80b9cd41e175a46be6f2a77d0bb66b02c95ce3e","intervals":[[1,84]],"path":"scripts/lib/test-narrow.d.mts"},{"blob":"05ed00b54a7a50c3e6aced453ad7465a1993f62d","intervals":[[1,356]],"path":"scripts/lib/test-narrow.mjs"}],"job_id":"00f2d14e1cc5ca4bcd3d091df89e8fa7ee30b061e317fd45e6bf674532637d08:0","outcome":"completed","paths":[{"baseline":{"me